artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a social organization modeled as a time-varying system consisting of (i) a set of agents that may enter and exit over time, (ii) a network topology on the current set of agents that may rewire and restructure, (iii) one or more dynamical processes running on the network (e.g., state variables attached to agents and edges that evolve via local interaction rules), and (iv) exogenous and endogenous shocks whose occurrence times may overlap the time scale of the ordinary dynamics so that shocks cannot be cleanly separated from "normal" evolution. Resilience of the organization is to be quantified from trajectories of this system (for instance, via the system's ability to maintain or recover specified structural and functional indicators after perturbations).

Problem: Identify and formalize the minimal collection of dynamical ingredients that any model of such a social organization must include in order to yield a well-defined and meaningful measure of resilience. Specifically, give a framework that simultaneously incorporates:
1. Concurrent evolution of network topology and of processes defined on the network;
2. Entry and exit of agents (open-population dynamics) and structural restructuring of the network;
3. Temporal paths and time-respecting connectivity, including the choice of appropriate aggregation time scales and overlapping or sliding time windows;
4. Internal and external shocks whose timing may coincide with or be indistinguishable from the regular dynamics;
and prove that the resulting resilience measure is well-defined, invariant under reasonable reparametrizations of time, and sensitive to each of the ingredients above.

